  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ on the interval $[-1,2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global maximum value is $13$\n- The global minimum value is $-7$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-18x+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =6x(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n6x(x-3)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0,\\,3.\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =13-9(-1)^{2}+2(-1)^{3}=2 \\\\ f(0) & =13-9(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}=13 \\\\ f(2) & =13-9(2)^{2}+2(2)^{3}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=13,$ so the global maximum is $13.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=-7,$ so the global minimum is $-7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/1a9d2f0db241c0a209c315a8e901e40a44258f339bbaa7bee36198b58fe2684d.png)",
